WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.789
Task statement 3.5.

2
00:00:01.789 --> 00:00:06.265
Apply iterative refinement techniques for progressive improvement.

3
00:00:06.265 --> 00:00:13.727
Three rounds of 'handle dates better' go nowhere; one message with four exact input and output pairs fixes it.

4
00:00:15.127 --> 00:00:19.505
Iterative refinement means improving a result over several short rounds.

5
00:00:19.505 --> 00:00:23.214
The rounds only help if each one gives Claude a clear signal.

6
00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:26.863
A vague comment like make it better gives nothing to aim at.

7
00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:34.647
A concrete example or a failing test is like a reference note when you tune a guitar: Claude can hear exactly how far off it is.

8
00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:38.593
Three techniques come up again and again.

9
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:42.443
First, show concrete inputs with the exact outputs you expect.

10
00:00:42.443 --> 00:00:47.163
Second, write or share failing tests and let Claude iterate until they pass.

11
00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:55.609
Third, for bigger features, ask Claude to interview you first, so hidden requirements and edge cases surface before any code is written.

12
00:00:57.009 --> 00:00:58.865
How you group feedback matters.

13
00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:05.269
If two problems share the same code path, send them together, because fixing one alone can break the other.

14
00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.920
If they are unrelated, fix them one at a time and check each.

15
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.656
Always name exact edge cases.

16
00:01:10.656 --> 00:01:16.103
And if you have corrected the same thing twice, start a clean session with a better prompt.

17
00:01:17.503 --> 00:01:20.426
A developer asks Claude to fix a date parser.

18
00:01:20.426 --> 00:01:24.648
Asking it to handle more formats gets a random spread of changes.

19
00:01:24.648 --> 00:01:29.131
Listing five real inputs with the expected output gets a focused fix.

20
00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:35.042
The timezone and daylight saving bugs live in the same function, so they go in one message.

21
00:01:35.042 --> 00:01:39.135
The CSV header bug is unrelated, so it waits for its own round.

22
00:01:40.535 --> 00:01:42.824
Most traps here are about vagueness.

23
00:01:42.824 --> 00:01:49.500
Prose where examples would do, generic phrases like handle edge cases, or the wrong grouping of feedback.

24
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.107
Ask yourself whether the issues interact.

25
00:01:52.107 --> 00:01:54.078
If they do, send them together.

26
00:01:54.078 --> 00:01:56.622
If they do not, take them one at a time.

27
00:01:56.622 --> 00:02:00.119
And always prefer a concrete example over an adjective.

28
00:02:01.519 --> 00:02:04.808
Refinement works when each round has a clear signal.

29
00:02:04.808 --> 00:02:11.323
Use examples instead of descriptions, failing tests as targets, and an interview before large features.

30
00:02:11.323 --> 00:02:13.980
Group feedback by whether issues interact.

31
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:19.609
Those habits turn a long, frustrating back and forth into a few short, productive rounds.

